    "title": "A pulsed point source's spherical wave",
    "description": "The scalar wave equation (1/c²) ∂²u/∂t² - ∇²u = f about an axis: sound in air (c = 343 m/s) from a Gaussian source of radius s = 2 cm pulsed for τ = 0.15 ms - a PDE of one's own, linear: its matrix factored once, each step one solve (600 steps of 10 µs in about half a minute). The outer arc absorbs it as an outgoing spherical wave: -n·Γ = g - q u with g = -ut/c, q = 1/R (∂u/∂R = -u̇/c - u/R), nothing coming back. The implicit midpoint rule keeps its energy: R u peaks at 0.93 at R = 0.25, 0.5 and 0.75 m (0.929 to 0.933, as its outputs 20 µs apart fall), at t0 + R/c - the analytic far field's τ/√(τ² + (s/c)²) = 0.9321 for a source of size s (a point's 1); BDF2 at this step loses half a percent of it each 0.25 m."
}